// PanicExitCode — the process exit code a SOFTWARE PANIC ends the process with, on EVERY target.
// One declaration, read by all three backends.
//
// ⚠ **IT WAS THREE DECLARATIONS OF ONE NUMBER, IN THREE FILES, UNDER TWO NAMES**: x64's
// `PanicExitCode = 1`, arm64's `RangePanicExitCode = 0x1` and wasm's `RangePanicExitCode = 1 as Imm32`
// — three spellings that cross-referenced each other BY COMMENT, and nothing checked that the comments
// were still true. A comment is the bottom rung of this project's cure ladder (DERIVE > CHECK >
// COMMENT), and the arm64 and wasm comments here had already gone stale about which lanes print a
// message. Read from here, the three backends cannot disagree about the number at all.
//
// ⭐ **THE NAME IS DELIBERATELY NOT `RangePanicExitCode`.** x64 spends this on EVERY terminal
// diagnostic `mrt_panic` emits — a range violation AND the two integer faults `idiv` raises — so it was
// never range-specific there. arm64 and wasm reach it only from their range guard, but that is because a
// range guard is the only software panic they lower, not because the code belongs to ranges. What the
// number identifies is "the process died in a panic", not which panic, so naming a reason in it would be
// false the moment a second panic path lands on either backend.
//
// ⚠ **WHY THIS IS NOT A `Runtime/RuntimeAbort` CASE**, though that enum is otherwise the one home of
// the exit codes emitted code dies with. Its cases are REASONS — a distinct number per guard, so a
// debugger reading an exit code lands in the right runtime function — and they are picked from the free
// space ABOVE 70 so that an abort is distinguishable from a program that merely returned. This number
// is the opposite on both counts: every panic shares it *deliberately* (there is no reason to read
// back), and it is not pickable at all — `specs-shv2/string-views.md` fixes it at a plain 1.
// ⚠ ITS ORIGINAL REASON IS SPENT: that sentence used to end "so the lanes with no panic runtime can
// assert on the exit code alone", and after W3 there is no such lane — every target prints a message.
// The number is still NOT pickable, for a reason that outlived the first: the wasm case pins it through
// the HARNESS (a trap pre-empts the guest's exit, so `wasmPanicReport` supplies `PanicExitCode` itself),
// so renumbering here silently changes what that lane asserts. Filing a spec-mandated, reason-free number in
// a registry whose stated rule is "take the next free number above 70" would tell the next reader it
// could be renumbered, which is the one thing it cannot be.
//
// UNTYPED, because the operand types differ and the VALUE does not: the two register ISAs load it as a
// `MachineWord` immediate, wasm pushes it as the `Imm32` of an `i32.const`. Three typed copies is the
// original defect with the conversions baked in; one untyped literal converted at its use is not.
export let PanicExitCode = 1
